erential equation \n\n$$\ny''  - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186175756 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = 7tx_{2} - e^{t}x_{1} - t\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t\n$$" 13194139535809 false] [17592186175756 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=7t{x}_{2}-{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t.\n$$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186175769 1100 "Consider the third-order differential equation\n\n$$\ny''' + t y' - 8 y = 1 - t^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $y = y(t).$ If\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the given differential equation can be rewritten as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf f(t),$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/2b3ba6edd838ee516ad902008726afbca516ab15892aad0f236c5a2270261985.png)\nDetermine the matrices $A$ and $\\mathbf{f}(t).$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186175769 1100 "Consider the third-order differential equation\n$y''' + t y' - 8 y = 1 - t^2$,\nwhere $y = y(t)$. If\n${y = x_{1}(t); y^{′} = x_{2}(t); y^{″} = x_{3}(t)$,\nthen the given differential equation can be rewritten as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nDetermine the matrices $A$ and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$." 13194139535809 false] [17592186175769 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{3} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′} &= y^{″} \\\\\nx_{3}^{′} &= y^{‴}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' =-t y' + 8 y + 1 - t^2\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' =-t x_2 + 8 x_1 + 1 - t^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = 8x_{1} - tx_{2} + 1 - t^{2}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′} \\\\ x_{2}^{′} \\\\ x_{3}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -t & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535809 false] [17592186175769 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′} & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = - t y' + 8 y + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = - t x_2 + 8 x_1 + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=8{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2}+1-{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/5a343acd288c1f6ddc72320b4eb17d9f24d3d41adebec579e70b9ff7ba17d0bb.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/dcb089aa64d2aeb959e6b33b03afecef75de96962a0468eb31b590ed6ecad6eb.png)" 13194139535809 true] [17592186176007 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367528 13194139535809 true] [17592186176007 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367535 13194139535809 true] [17592186176007 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367542 13194139535809 true] [17592186176007 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367549 13194139535809 true] [17592186176008 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367567 13194139535809 true] [17592186176008 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367576 13194139535809 true] [17592186176008 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367589 13194139535809 true] [17592186176144 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535809 true] [17592186176144 1100 "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y^{‴} - \\sqrt{t}y^{″} - y = t^{2}$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$ and $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." 13194139535809 false] [17592186176144 1100 "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}-y={t}^{2}\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186176144 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},$ ${x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}+y+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{x}_{1}+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={x}_{1}+\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)={x}_{1}+\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186176388 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535809 true] [17592186176388 1100 "The second-order differential equation $y^{″} + 6y^{′} - y = \\sin t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$, where\n\n| $x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, A =$ |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  | $, f(t) =$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $1$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y = y(t)$, we have $y = x_{1}(t)$ and $y^{′} = x_{2}(t)$." 13194139535809 false] [17592186176388 1100 "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $1$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t).$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186176388 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}+y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-6{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186176389 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535809 true] [17592186176389 1100 "The third-order differential equation $y^{‴} - ty^{″} + e^{t}y^{′} = 2$ can be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$, where\n\n| $x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}, A =$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  | $, f(t) =$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $-e^{t}$ | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y = y(t)$, we have $y = x_{1}(t)$, $y^{′} = x_{2}(t)$, and $y^{″} = x_{3}(t)$." 13194139535809 false] [17592186176389 1100 "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-t{y}^{″}+{e}^{t}{y}^{′}=2$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  | $,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $-{e}^{t}$ | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t),$ ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t),$ and ${y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).$" 13194139535809 true] [17592186176389 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following ch